{
 "id": "E03",
 "title": "Who Controls the Signal That Finds You?",
 "world": "orbit",
 "fps": 30,
 "seed": "s02",
 "passages": [
  {
   "id": "P01",
   "text": "Look at the blue dot on your phone. It knows where you are. It can guide a driver through an unfamiliar city. It can help a ship approach a port. It can keep a clock in a network synchronised.",
   "estSec": 21.7,
   "flag": "",
   "words": 39
  },
  {
   "id": "P02",
   "text": "But the dot raises a question most people never ask. Who owns the signal that makes it possible?",
   "estSec": 36.1,
   "flag": "",
   "words": 65
  },
  {
   "id": "P03",
   "text": "India has said the concern plainly: civilian signals from foreign navigation systems can potentially be degraded or denied. That does not prove every dramatic story told about a past shutdown. It explains why an independent system matters.",
   "estSec": 20.6,
   "flag": "",
   "words": 37
  },
  {
   "id": "P04",
   "text": "So India developed NavIC, Navigation with Indian Constellation, a regional satellite navigation system.",
   "estSec": 38.3,
   "flag": "",
   "words": 69
  },
  {
   "id": "P05",
   "text": "The satellite broadcasts its position and a precise time reference. Your receiver compares that time with the arrival of the signal. The difference helps estimate distance. But one distance is not enough. It tells you that you could be somewhere on a huge imaginary sphere around the satellite.",
   "estSec": 26.7,
   "flag": "",
   "words": 48
  },
  {
   "id": "P06",
   "text": "Now add another satellite. Then another. Their distance measurements narrow the possible locations. The receiver's clock is not as precise as the satellites' clocks. So more measurements are needed, to solve for position and clock error together.",
   "estSec": 20.6,
   "flag": "",
   "words": 37
  },
  {
   "id": "P07",
   "text": "Navigation is also a story about time.",
   "estSec": 25,
   "flag": "",
   "words": 45
  },
  {
   "id": "P08",
   "text": "NavIC gave India a regional navigation architecture under its own control. Its signals can also work alongside other global navigation systems in compatible receivers.",
   "estSec": 51.1,
   "flag": "",
   "words": 92
  },
  {
   "id": "P09",
   "text": "Here is a fictional scenario. A fishing vessel receives signals from several navigation systems. One signal becomes unreliable. A compatible receiver can compare the remaining measurements, but only if enough usable signals are available. Resilience comes from both independence and redundancy.",
   "estSec": 22.8,
   "flag": "FICTIONAL SCENARIO",
   "words": 41
  },
  {
   "id": "P10",
   "text": "The blue dot looked simple. Behind it was a network of clocks, orbits and decisions.",
   "estSec": 28.3,
   "flag": "",
   "words": 51
  }
 ],
